{"qid":"Q45","names":["Portuguese Republic","Portugal"],"title":"Portuguese Republic","endonym":{"native":"Portugal","lang":"pt"},"capsule":"The First Portuguese Republic (1910–1926) replaced the constitutional monarchy after a republican revolution on 5 October 1910 deposed King Manuel II. The republic was characterized by chronic political instability—sixteen years produced eight presidents, forty-five governments, and several military uprisings—while maintaining Portugal's colonial empire in Africa and Asia, which it defended against German encroachment during the First World War. Fiscal crisis and civil disorder culminated in the coup of 28 May 1926, which installed a military dictatorship; this eventually gave way to António de Oliveira Salazar's Estado Novo from 1933.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Metropolitan Portugal's boundaries are precisely attested; colonial possessions during this period are well-mapped from administrative records, though some African boundary demarcation was still being finalized in the 1910s.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Portuguese First Republic","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portuguese_First_Republic"},{"label":"Britannica: Portugal — The First Republic","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Portugal/The-First-Republic-1910-26"},{"label":"Britannica: António de Oliveira Salazar","url":"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Antonio-de-Oliveira-Salazar"}],"britannica":"place/Portugal","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Portugal","rulers":[{"qid":"Q345926","name":"Teófilo Braga","start":1910,"end":1911,"image":"Teófilo Braga (1915) - Fotografia Vasque